![An HP notebook keyboard](cover.jpg)

Image: [Wikimedia Commons](https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:HP_laptop_keyboard.jpg)

The other day a computer came in for repair — an _HP 14 Laptop_. I
think keyboard failures are common in modern notebooks.

But it is not just any failure: usually the keyboard works fine
except that some keys stop responding. Keys in one row.

For example, this keyboard worked for every key except the `zxcv`
row.

Booting from a USB stick with a Linux system did not help — the
keyboard still failed, so it was not a driver or configuration
issue.

The trouble I keep finding is that some notebook models,
especially the cheap ones, place the battery right over the
keyboard's connection cable.

This particular notebook had a lot of dust under the battery and
over the keyboard cable, which caused interference when sending
signals.

The recommendation is simply to clean the cable with isopropyl
alcohol and give the notebook and its components a general
cleaning.

Following an official manufacturer's manual, it is easy to know
which screwdrivers to use and which screws to remove to open the
notebook.

Before thinking about buying a new machine, a general cleaning is
always worth doing — maybe it is just dirt keeping our electronics
from working properly.
